# Accessibility

- Use native semantic controls before recreating controls with gestures or generic containers.
- Give interactive controls clear accessible names, roles, states, focus order, and keyboard paths.
- Mark purely decorative artwork as hidden from assistive technology when adjacent text conveys it.
- Announce asynchronous errors, success, progress, and important state changes without stealing
  focus.
- Preserve Dynamic Type or scalable text, RTL layout, high contrast, reduced motion, and usable
  target sizes.
- Never encode meaning only through color, position, animation, or an unlabeled icon.
- Test the affected flow with platform accessibility inspection and keyboard navigation when the
  environment supports it; record any device-only gap.
